    burden: "About 1 percent of colorectal cancers: 11,510 of 1,087,055 patients (1.06 percent) in the meta-analysis (Discover Oncology 2021) and 160 of 28,056 (0.6 percent) in the Munich registry, diagnoses 1998 to 2012 (J Cancer Res Clin Oncol 2016).",
    tldr: "Signet ring cell carcinoma is a rare form of bowel cancer, about one in a hundred, in which the cells fill with mucus that pushes the nucleus aside, like a ring. It favours the right colon, is usually found late and often spreads across the lining of the abdomen. In a German registry five-year survival was 40 percent against 59 for ordinary bowel cancer. It is treated like other bowel cancer.",
    summary: "Signet ring cell carcinoma is by definition composed of at least 50 percent of cells with signet ring morphology (Pathology Oncology Research 2021); the published summary of the 2019 WHO classification (Nagtegaal 2020) does not describe the type. The review evidence associates it with young age, proximal site, advanced stage, high grade, frequent node involvement, peritoneal metastasis and significantly shorter survival; molecularly, the classic WNT, RAS-MAPK and PI3K pathways are mutated less often than in ordinary adenocarcinoma while RNF43, CDH1, SMAD4 and TGF-beta pathway genes are altered more often, and many but not all studies report more BRAF mutation and microsatellite instability (Pathology Oncology Research 2021). In the meta-analysis, signet ring cell patients were slightly younger (a weighted mean difference of 0.47 years) and twice as likely to have right-sided disease (odds ratio 2.12), and five-year overall survival was worse than adenocarcinoma (hazard ratio 2.54, 95 percent confidence interval 1.98 to 3.27; the figure for mucinous carcinoma was 1.38) (Discover Oncology 2021). In the Munich registry, diagnoses 1998 to 2012, 76 percent were in the proximal colon, it was diagnosed at more advanced stages, and five-year overall survival was 40 percent against 59 percent for ordinary adenocarcinoma, although histology was not an independent factor once stage and other features were taken into account (J Cancer Res Clin Oncol 2016).\n\nHow it differs from its parent: it had the lowest five-year survival of the three histologies in the Munich registry (40 percent, against 52 for mucinous and 59 for ordinary adenocarcinoma); it spreads to the peritoneum more often and to the liver and lung less often than ordinary adenocarcinoma; and CDH1, the gene commonly mutated in signet ring cell carcinoma of the stomach, is mutated in a proportion of cases (Pathology Oncology Research 2021).\n\nHow common: about 1 percent (Discover Oncology 2021).\n\nTreatment: as the parent by stage and mismatch repair status; peritoneal disease follows the colorectal page's approach to peritoneal metastases. The review cites a study of 1,972 patients in which adjuvant fluorouracil-based chemotherapy for stage III colon cancer gave similar benefit in signet ring and ordinary tumours; the sources read report no trial in the type.",
    subtypes: ["Signet ring cell carcinoma, mismatch-repair deficient (MSI-high; right-sided)", "Signet ring cell carcinoma, mismatch-repair proficient (CDH1, RNF43 and SMAD4 altered; peritoneal spread)", "Mucinous adenocarcinoma with signet ring cells under 50 percent (classified as mucinous)"],
    biomarkers: ["Signet ring cells over 50 percent of the tumour", "Mismatch repair status", "CDH1, RNF43 and SMAD4 alterations (research)", "Peritoneal disease on imaging"],
